                <h2>This Week's Observation: Three Signs That Tiered Management Is Urgent</h2>
<p>This week, Yiyu Digital Media noticed three notable changes while serving clients:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Signal 1</strong>: Meta's review team strengthened monitoring of new accounts' first-week投放 behavior. Multiple teams reported being flagged by the system for high-frequency budget adjustments.</li>
<li><strong>Signal 2</strong>: TikTok's ad spend cap mechanism was adjusted again this week, rendering some multi-account teams' daily budget allocation logic ineffective.</li>
<li><strong>Signal 3</strong>: A tool-app team had 4 ad accounts banned in a batch due to account association issues, resulting in over $8,000 in lost balance.</li>
</ul>
<p>These three signals point to the same issue—<strong>unclear account tiering is creating increasingly high operational risks and costs</strong>.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Core Problem Analysis: Three Typical Scenarios of Tiering Chaos</h2>
<h3>Scenario 1: "If It Works, Use It" — No Functional Tiering</h3>
<p>Many teams use a "saturation投放" approach in early business stages, opening numerous accounts to run materials in parallel and quickly testing which ones scale. This works during the growth phase but creates serious problems in the maintenance phase:</p>
<ul>
<li>Main accounts and test accounts are mixed, polluting the overall投放 model with low-quality test data</li>
<li>Account balances are scattered, preventing concentrated resources for high-converting materials</li>
<li>When accounts are banned, root causes are hard to trace because all accounts served the same function</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Recommendation</strong>: Classify accounts by function into three tiers: "Main Scale Account," "Material Test Account," and "Brand Display Account." Set independent budget limits and review standards for each tier.</p>
<h3>Scenario 2: Top-Up Strategy Doesn't Match Account Tiers</h3>
<p>A cross-border e-commerce team we served this week faced this exact issue: they had 6 Google Ads accounts, 2 mature accounts running blockbuster products and 4 new accounts testing new products. Yet all accounts had the same top-up cycle, causing mature accounts to frequently中断投放 due to insufficient balance while new accounts' testing costs consumed significant cash flow.</p>
<p><strong>Recommendation</strong>: Establish differentiated top-up strategies based on account tiers—mature accounts maintain 7 days of balance redundancy, new accounts receive weekly supplements based on actual testing consumption to avoid capital being tied up in low-efficiency accounts.</p>
<h3>Scenario 3: Discovering Account Attribution Chaos During Reconciliation</h3>
<p>This is the most overlooked issue for multi-account teams. Many teams only discover during month-end reconciliation that:</p>
<ul>
<li>Certain top-ups were charged additional account maintenance fees</li>
<li>Some accounts' spend data differs from backend reports</li>
<li>Balance from banned accounts cannot be traced</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Recommendation</strong>: Assign each account a unique identification number, strongly bind top-up records with account numbers, and establish weekly reconciliation mechanisms rather than handling everything at month-end.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Action Checklist: Three Key Steps for Account Tiering</h2>
<ol>
<li>
<p><strong>Account Classification and Tagging</strong><br />
   Add clear business labels to each account (e.g., "NA-Tools-Main"), and record account creation date, historical ban count, and core KPI data in the ad platform backend.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Budget Allocation Ratio Reference</strong></p>
</li>
<li>Main accounts: 60-70% of total budget, priority for sufficient balance</li>
<li>Test accounts: 20-30% of total budget, set daily/weekly spend caps</li>
<li>
<p>Backup accounts: 10% of total budget, only activated when main accounts show anomalies</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Establish Early Warning Mechanisms</strong><br />
   Set up account health checklists: balance below 3 days of spend, review rejection rate exceeding 15%, single-account ROAS declining for 5 consecutive days—any trigger warrants immediate investigation.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>This Week's Actionable Recommendations</h2>
<p>If you're managing 3 or more ad accounts, this week consider conducting an account audit: map each account's functional positioning, top-up cycle, and health status. The essence of tiered management isn't increasing management costs—it's <strong>reducing hidden losses from management chaos</strong>.</p>
<p>Yiyu Digital Media will continue observing投放 policy changes across major ad platforms next week, providing timely account management recommendations for overseas teams.</p>
